    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the value of \\(a\\), the smallest positive two-digit multiple of \\(3\\), we start by identifying the smallest two-digit number, which is \\(10\\). We need to check if \\(10\\) is a multiple of \\(3\\):\n\\[10 \\div 3 = 3 \\text{ remainder } 1\\]\nSince the remainder is \\(1\\), \\(10\\) is not a multiple of \\(3\\). The next two-digit number is \\(11\\), and we check:\n\\[11 \\div 3 = 3 \\text{ remainder } 2\\]\nSince the remainder is \\(2\\), \\(11\\) is not a multiple of \\(3\\). The next two-digit number is \\(12\\), and we check:\n\\[12 \\div 3 = 4 \\text{ remainder } 0\\]\nSince the remainder is \\(0\\), \\(12\\) is a multiple of \\(3\\). Therefore, the smallest positive two-digit multiple of \\(3\\) is \\(12\\). So, \\(a = 12\\).\n\nNext, to find the value of \\(b\\), the smallest positive three-digit multiple of \\(4\\), we start by identifying the smallest three-digit number, which is \\(100\\). We need to check if \\(100\\) is a multiple of \\(4\\):\n\\[100 \\div 4 = 25\\]\nSince the division is exact, \\(100\\) is a multiple of \\(4\\). Therefore, the smallest positive three-digit multiple of \\(4\\) is \\(100\\). So, \\(b = 100\\).\n\nNow, we need to find the sum \\(a + b\\):\n\\[a + b = 12 + 100 = 112\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{112}\n\\]",
